} "To accommodate a new women’s division, this category was now for boxers weighing 47-50 kg, whereas in Tokyo the weight range was 48-51 kg. Reigning Olympic Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/122371"} "Stoyka Krasteva"] " of Bulgaria had retired following her victory in Tokyo, so there would be a new champion. China’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/151034"} "Wu Yu"] " was the reigning World Champion and top seed, and was expected to win the division. This she did comfortably, defeating the current World Champion at light-flyweight, " [:a {:href "/athletes/151079"} "Nikhat Zareen"] " of India, in round two by a unanimous decision."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Wu then had a unanimous decision against Thailand’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/151134"} "Chuthamat Raksat"] " in the quarter-finals, and a split 4-1 decision against Kazakh " [:a {:href "/athletes/151094"} "Nazym Kyzaibay"] " in the semi-final. Wu’s opponent in the final was Turk " [:a {:href "/athletes/146919"} "Buse Naz Çakıroğlu"] ", silver medalist from 2020, who also settled for the silver medal in Paris, as Wu won the final by a split 4-1 decision. The other losing semi-finalist was Filipino " [:a {:href "/athletes/151117"} "Aira Villegas"] ", whose only previous international success had been as a bronze medalist at the 2019 Southeast Asia Games."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/151034"} "Wu Yu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/146919"} "Buse Naz Çakıroğlu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TUR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "http